in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I take app engines new scheduling  flags for a ride will we buck the  performance trend stay tuned to find out  now one of the nice aspects of the cloud  being a platform is that over time it'll  grow change and improve itself which is  exactly why we're talking about app  engine instance count again see since  our episode last year the App Engine  team rolled out a brand new scheduling  system to help you get even more control  over how and when App Engine spins up  instances so of course I wanted to dive  in kick the tires and see how some of  the new settings works but first I  wanted to get a baseline read on how the  scheduler responds in the same tests  that we use last time once again our  server code is pretty simple it's just a  simulation of a CPU intensive  application obviously if you have a four  second response time you've got bigger  problems to worry about as a baseline  here is 1k queries per second which the  previous scheduler shows differences  between the new and now we can see that  without touching any settings the new  scheduler is already performing just a  little bit better for my contrived  scenario which is good because it means  that without lifting a finger we get a  little better on instance counts next is  something I've been interested in for a  while  min and Max instance settings max  instances of course defines the upper  limit on the number of instances you can  have which is helpful to control your  costs while min instances ensures that  after deployment there's always a  certain number of instances running so  but before you set this flag make sure  you take into account how your minimum  instance account interacts with your  idle instance account since they are two  very different concepts and you don't  want a lot of spare instances lying  around when you don't need them out make  sure you check out the article in the  description below for more details on  that  anyhow next up is the target throughput  utilization flag which sets a threshold  for the number of concurrent requests  after which more instances will be  started to handle the traffic this flag  works hand in hand with max concurrent  requests to determine your true number  of requests per instance for throughput  heavy applications lowering this value  will result in more instances being spun  up it's you handled the work while  increasing this value will let each in  better handle the connections and in  most cases should reduce incidence count  now finally is the target CPU  utilization flag this flag defines the  CPU utilization ratio which defines when  a new instance should be spun up or  perhaps a better way to say it if your  app is CPU bound then this is the  parameter you tweak to optimize between  performance and costs since lowering and  raising it can impact how many instances  you spin up in order to handle the  workload with an improved scheduler and  a new set of settings App Engine gives  you much better controls to better  balance between performance and costs  which in my humble opinion is always the  spot you want to be in if you'd like to  know more about these tests check out  the article linked in the description  below and don't forget to check out the  rest of the cloud performance Atlas  content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

